The design below assumes a multi-tenant reporting service, sensitive report data, and plugins written in more than one language.

# 1. System architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Publisher[Plugin build pipeline] --> Catalog[Signed package catalog]
    Catalog --> Installer[Installation manager]
    Installer --> Validator[Schema, signature, SBOM and policy validation]
    Validator --> Registry[(Plugin registry)]

    Registry --> Resolver[Capability resolver]
    Resolver --> Router[Invocation router]
    Registry --> Supervisor[Runtime supervisor]

    Supervisor --> R1[WASI sandbox]
    Supervisor --> R2[Isolated process]
    Supervisor --> R3[OCI sandbox]

    Router <--> R1
    Router <--> R2
    Router <--> R3

    R1 <--> Broker[Host capability broker]
    R2 <--> Broker
    R3 <--> Broker

    Broker --> Data[Report data services]
    Broker --> Storage[Scoped plugin storage]
    Broker --> Secrets[Secrets broker]
    Broker --> Network[Egress proxy]
    Broker --> Telemetry[Logs, metrics and traces]
```

The platform has two planes:

- Control plane: packages, installation, signatures, configuration, permissions, activation, version selection, and audit history.
- Execution plane: sandbox processes, request routing, lifecycle coordination, quotas, health checks, and host-service brokering.

There must be no in-process plugin mechanism, including for “trusted” first-party plugins. Maintaining one boundary avoids two security models and prevents internal plugins from accidentally depending on private host behavior.

# 2. Plugin package

Use an immutable `.rptplugin` ZIP archive:

```text
manifest.json
payload/
  ...
schemas/
  config.schema.json
  config-ui.schema.json
sbom/
  sbom.cdx.json
licenses/
  ...
META-INF/
  files.sha256
  signature.dsse.json
```

Rules:

- Every published version is immutable and addressed by SHA-256 digest.
- `files.sha256` covers every file except the signature envelope.
- The signature covers the manifest and hash index.
- Package ID plus semantic version is unique.
- Rebuilding the same version with different bytes is rejected.
- Runtime dependencies must be included in the package or sandbox image.
- Package signatures identify a publisher, not merely a build pipeline.
- Revocation is supported by publisher, version, signing key, and package digest.

