I’m assuming a server-side, multi-tenant reporting platform. Desktop deployments can use the same contracts with local workers.

# 1. Target architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    DEV[Plugin CI/CD] -->|signed OCI artifact| AR[Artifact Registry]
    ADMIN[Admin / Control Plane] --> PR[Plugin Registry]
    PR --> AR
    PR --> ORCH[Plugin Orchestrator]

    APP[Reporting Engine] --> GW[Invocation Gateway]
    GW --> ORCH
    ORCH --> WP[Sandboxed Plugin Worker]

    WP --> SB[Secret Broker]
    WP --> DB[Data Access Broker]
    WP --> ST[State / Blob Broker]
    WP --> EG[Egress Proxy]

    ORCH --> OBS[Logs / Metrics / Traces / Audit]
    GW --> OBS
    WP --> OBS
```

The architecture has six distinct responsibilities:

| Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Artifact registry | Stores immutable, signed plugin releases by digest |
| Plugin registry | Catalog, releases, installations, grants, configurations, activation state |
| Orchestrator | Compatibility resolution, lifecycle, worker placement, upgrades and rollback |
| Invocation gateway | Authentication, routing, deadlines, quotas, retries and circuit breaking |
| Sandboxed worker | Executes exactly one plugin release under restricted permissions |
| Capability brokers | Mediate secrets, storage, data access, blobs and network egress |

A plugin must never:

- Execute during discovery or installation.
- Run inside the reporting engine process.
- Receive host credentials or database connection strings.
- Mount host directories.
- Call another plugin directly.
- Trust tenant, identity, or permission information supplied by plugin configuration.

# 2. Supported extension points

Use separately versioned contracts for each extension point.

| Extension | Inputs | Outputs | Important restrictions |
|---|---|---|---|
| `data-source` | Query plan, parameters, principal context | Arrow record batches | Data access through broker when possible |
| `transform` | Arrow streams plus transformation config | Arrow streams | Determinism declared in manifest |
| `renderer` | Report model, theme, locale | SVG, PDF, PNG, or sanitized render tree | Active HTML disabled by default |
| `exporter` | Report model or rendered blob | Blob handle plus metadata | Bounded output size |
| `destination` | Blob handle and delivery request | Durable delivery receipt | Must implement idempotency |
| `formula` | Typed scalar/vector arguments | Typed results | WASM-only, no network or secrets |
| `metadata-provider` | Search/schema request | Catalog metadata | Read-only by default |

Authentication providers, policy engines, and arbitrary UI plugins should require a higher trust tier and separate contracts. They should not be slipped into the generic plugin API.

Plugin-to-plugin dependencies should be prohibited in API v1. Pipelines compose extensions through the host, preventing dependency cycles and transitive permission escalation.

# 3. Package format

Store releases as OCI artifacts so existing registries, digest pinning, signatures, SBOMs, and vulnerability tooling can be reused.

Example package:

```text
plugin/
├── plugin.json
├── schemas/
│   ├── config.schema.json
│   └── config.ui.json
├── artifacts/
│   └── plugin.wasm
├── migrations/
│   └── 2-to-3.json
├── docs/
│   └── README.md
├── sbom.spdx.json
└── provenance.json
```

Native/container plugins use an OCI image as their executable artifact. WASM plugins use an OCI layer with a dedicated media type.

Releases are addressed only by digest in installations:

```text
registry.example.com/report-plugins/acme/snowflake@sha256:...
```

Tags such as `1.4.2` or `stable` are discovery conveniences and must never be activation identities.

Sign the artifact digest externally. Do not put a signature or package digest inside `plugin.json`, because that creates circular integrity semantics.

